Package: anovakit
Title: Analysis of Variance Workflows with Assumption Checks and Effect Sizes
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: person("Justin", "Chase", email = "jchase.msu@gmail.com",
                  role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Provides a consistent set of wrappers around the analysis of
    variance designs that applied work most often needs: Welch's analysis of
    variance (Welch, 1951, <doi:10.1093/biomet/38.3-4.330>), the
    Kruskal-Wallis test with Dunn post-hoc comparisons (Dunn, 1964,
    <doi:10.1080/00401706.1964.10490181>), analysis of covariance, repeated
    measures with the Greenhouse-Geisser (1959,
    <doi:10.1007/BF02289823>) and Huynh-Feldt (1976,
    <doi:10.3102/10769986001001069>) corrections, multivariate analysis of
    variance and covariance with Mardia's normality tests (Mardia, 1970,
    <doi:10.1093/biomet/57.3.519>) and Box's M (Box, 1949,
    <doi:10.1093/biomet/36.3-4.317>), and analyses of deviance for binary and
    count responses. Each function validates its input, checks the assumptions
    that matter for the method it implements, reports effect sizes with
    confidence intervals, computes estimated marginal means and pairwise
    comparisons, and returns 'ggplot2' objects rather than drawing them. All
    eight functions share one argument convention and return one S3 class with
    'print', 'summary' and 'plot' methods.
